Trunks, the stems of woody plants, connect the [[root]]s to the upper [[branches]], [[Canopy (biology)|canopy]], and [[leaves]]. In general, the trunk of woody plants, which is their most easily identifiable feature, consists of: heartwood, sapwood, [[cambium]], inner bark, outer bark, and the pith.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=20}}{{Sfn|Wiedenhoeft|Miller|2005|p=11}} In this way, the [[xylem]], or wood, of the tree is separated from the bark by the cambium, which functions as a lateral [[meristem]]. The cambium promotes growth radially.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=19}}{{Sfn|Bernatzky|1978|p=20}} The younger part of the xylem (the sapwood) conducts water up from the roots to the leaves. It also acts as storage for food, through the [[parenchyma]], which is made up of [[ray cell]]s.{{Sfn|Wiedenhoeft|Miller|2005|p=12}} While only 10% of the sapwood cells are alive, the heartwood, the darker part of the xylem, is completely dead. It proves structural value to the plant.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=20}}{{Sfn|Bernatzky|1978|p=19}}{{Sfn|Wiedenhoeft|Miller|2005|p=12}} The [[pith]] is the most minor feature of the trunk, being a remnant from when the stem was not yet woody.{{Sfn|Wiedenhoeft|Miller|2005|p=11}} The purpose of producing a trunk is to enable a taller plant, with greater stability.{{Sfn|Plavcová|Gallenmüller|Morris|Khatamirad|2019|p=3690}}

Earlywood and latewood describe the difference in density between wood grown early (low density) and later (high density) in the growing season.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=21}} [[Dendrochronology|Tree rings]], seen when the trunk is viewed in cross-section, are the result of the difference in cambial growth rates during the year. The difference in thickness of the cells of earlywood and latewood is generally responsible for the presence of growth rings. They are most pronounced in [[conifers]] and are mostly not annual in [[equator]]ial regions.{{Sfn|Creber|1977|pp=349–350}} In [[angiosperms]], annual rings are also influenced by the proportion of different cells present in the different regions. This varies between [[genera]], however.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=20}} The outer annual ring or rings are generally responsible for most of the water transport in trees, to differing degrees.{{Sfn|Bernatzky|1978|p=19}}{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=314}}

=== In gymnosperms ===
{{See also|Gymnosperms}}

Up to 90% of the xylem of gymnosperms is made up of vertically oriented [[tracheids]], a type of conductive cell, which often overlap one another.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=24}} To facilitate liquid transfer, the cell walls of tracheids contain [[Pit (botany)|pits]], and are around 100 times as long as they are wide.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=24}}{{Sfn|Dahle|Grabosky|2009|p=312}}{{Sfn|Wiedenhoeft|Miller|2005|p=21}} They also provide structural strength through their thick [[cell wall]].{{Sfn|Dahle|Grabosky|2009|p=312}} In the horizontal (or radial) direction, the most significant component in gymnosperms are [[Medullary ray (botany)|wood rays]], formed by the cambium. They consist of groups of cells which both store [[carbohydrates]] and [[minerals]], but also move water, minerals, and other compounds in the horizontal direction. Ray tracheids and [[parenchyma]], in different combinations, make up the structure of wood rays.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=25}} Parenchyma chiefly function as nutrient storage, but can also assist in liquid transport to a limited degree. They also supply mechanical strength to the tree.{{Sfn|Plavcová|Gallenmüller|Morris|Khatamirad|2019|p=3690}}

=== In angiosperms ===
{{See also|Angiosperms}}
In angiosperms, the axial direction is dominated by fibres, as well as [[vessel element]]s, parenchyma cells and tracheids (both vascular and vasicentric), as in gymnosperms. The vessel elements are responsible for the majority of water transport and as such are orientated on top of one-another.{{Sfn|Dahle|Grabosky|2009|p=312}} They range from 1 to 10 m in length and the presence of them can be used to separate [[hardwood]]s from [[softwood]]s.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=26}}{{Sfn|Wiedenhoeft|Miller|2005|p=24}} The structure of fibres is similar to tracheids, but with smaller pits and thicker cell walls. Their main function is structural.{{Sfn|Wiedenhoeft|Miller|2005|p=25}} Generally, the proportion of axial parenchyma found in angiosperms is greater than that found in gymnosperms.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=26}} In the horizontal direction, wood rays can be found, as in gymnosperms, however they consist exclusively of parenchyma.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=27}} In contrast to gymnosperms, the radial water transport is mostly achieved through adjacent axial vessels, or between any axial member through their pits.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=26}}

=== Bark ===
{{See also|Bark (botany)}}
The structure of bark consists of a primary [[phloem]], secondary phloem, [[Cortex (botany)|cortex]], [[periderm]] and a dead outer layer of rhytidome. This is the case for radial growth caused by the cambium, called [[secondary growth]]. In [[primary growth]] located at stem tips, however, the secondary phloem and periderm are not grown. Phloem support carbohydrate transport throughout the tree, through a process called translocation. The periderm protects the trunk from mechanical damage and reduces loss of water.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=27}} [[Lenticel]]s are small holes in the periderm consisting of porous tissue that allow for gas transfer.{{Sfn|Lendzian|2006|p=2536}} This includes transfer of [[carbon dioxide]], [[oxygen]], and water.{{Sfn|Lendzian|2006|p=2542}}

== Growth ==
{{Main|Primary growth|Secondary growth}}
[[File:Tree secondary growth diagram.svg|thumb|Diagram showing secondary growth]]
There are two types of growth that produce tree trunks: primary (vertical) growth of stems, and secondary (radial) growth through the cambium. Primary growth occurs on the [[apical meristems]] through [[apical dominance]], in which buds not at the tip are prevented from growing.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=53}}{{Sfn|Tognetti|Bielach|Hrtyan|2017|p=2586}}

Secondary growth occurs in the vascular part of the cambium, in the cambial zone, a layer between 1 and 10 cells thick. Both additive and multiplicative division take place in this zone. In additive division, [[fusiform]] (thin but wide in the middle) initial cells (initials) are tangentially divided to produce mother cells for the subsequent production of xylem and phloem cells. In multiplicative division, the same initial cells are divided anticlinally (in perpendicular direction to neighbouring cells). This is the division responsible for growing the diameter of the trunk.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=55}}{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4499}}

When trees grow on a lean, it causes an increase in density and cambial growth in the concave section being leaned on. The wood this creates is called [[reaction wood]] and is generally undesirable. In angiosperms it is known as tension wood and in gymnosperms as compression wood, as a result of the different strategies (or reactions) employed by the trees.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=61}}{{Sfn|Shigo|1986|p=90}}{{sfn|James|Moore|Slater|Dahle|2018|p=5}}

=== Hormones ===
[[Auxin]] is the hormone responsible for preventing auxiliary buds from growing, thus fostering apical dominance.{{Sfn|Tognetti|Bielach|Hrtyan|2017|p=2587}} The exact mechanism and full picture of its contribution, as well as genetic and other factors is not clear.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=53}}

Although all of the major plant [[growth hormones]] can be found in the cambium region, auxin exerts a major influence on both divisions that occur in the cambial zone. There is some evidence that [[gibberellins]] have an effect on cambial growth in some plants.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=58}} There is evidence to suggest that exogenous [[cytokinin]]s both stimulate and do not stimulate cambial growth rates.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=59}} [[Abscisic acid]] (ABA) has an effect on cambial growth, although it is not clear in what way. [[Ethylene]] has been found to contribute to controlling the amount of xylem or phloem cells being produced by trees.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=59}} [[Ethephon]] (or ethrel) has been shown to effect the sizes of xylem and phloem cells and cell walls, depending on its concentration.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=60}} Both [[Indole-3-acetic acid]] (IAA) and ABA have a variable effect on tree trunk growth, depending on the time of year.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|p=60}}

=== Wounding ===
If a tree trunk is damaged either mechanically or chemically a wound can be produced, which increases the risk of disease through [[pathogen]]s.{{Sfn|Bernatzky|1978|p=21}} In response, the sapwood creates a barrier of discoloured wood which contains extractives. Extractives are special [[molecule]]s found but not attached (extraneous) to cell walls.{{Sfn|Morel-Rouhier|2021|p=125}} The effect of this is to inhibit the movement of pathogens or other [[Microorganism|micro-organisms]]. If broken through, the tree will further block motion using thicker-walled cells, [[tyloses]], or by plugging vessels, depending on the [[species]]. In general, wounds generate a complex [[Biochemistry|biochemical]] and [[Physiology|physiological]] response which is not fully understood. To eventually heal a wound, the trees produce [[callus tissue]] that is later converted into new cambial cells.{{Sfn|Pallardy|Kozlowski|2008|pp=67–68}}

== Mechanics ==
[[File:Dynamic Tree Model.svg|thumb|496x496px|Simple dynamic model of a tree, where k and c are the [[stiffness]] and [[damping]], respectively]]
Forces imposed by wind affect both the growth and structure of trees. They result in internal forces ([[Stress (mechanics)|stress]]) and elongations ([[Strain (mechanics)|strain]]), as well as [[vibration]]s. To [[Adaptation|adapt]] and [[Evolution|evolve]] to face these, tree trunks have an internal structure that resists [[oscillation]] and fracture.{{sfn|James|Moore|Slater|Dahle|2018|p=6}} Static (stationary) analysis provides a basis for understanding the effects of [[Weight|self weight]] and wind, while dynamic (moving) analysis describes a more accurate depiction of wind loading.{{sfn|James|Dahle|Grabosky|Kane|2014|p=2}}

=== Statics ===
The structure of wood is such that it can neither be called totally [[Elasticity (physics)|elastic]] (spring-like) or totally [[Viscosity|viscous]] (fluid-like), and therefore it is described as [[Viscoelasticity|viscoelastic]] (somewhere in between).{{sfn|James|Dahle|Grabosky|Kane|2014|p=2}} In addition to this, wood is not [[Isotropy|isotropic]] (the same in all directions) like traditionally studied materials such as [[metal]]s and also behaves in a [[Nonlinear system|non-linear]] way.{{sfn|James|Dahle|Grabosky|Kane|2014|p=2}}{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4498}} This is as a result of different cell orientations and the angles of [[microfibril]]s in the cell walls.{{Sfn|Lachenbruch|McCulloh|2014|p=751}} This, together with other variable factors such as the [[moisture content]] and [[turgor pressure]] (force exerted by water in plants), make most conventional engineering analysis not applicable.{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4498}}  Simplifying the structure of tree trunks for analysis can be done in three ways. One way is to treat them as a [[composite material]], in which tracheids and fibres bear most of the load. Another is to consider them to be a multilamellar composite, where each unit contains one or more [[Basal lamina|laminae]].{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4501}} Each of these is then said to be a composite material consisting of microfibrils of [[cellulose]] embedded in either [[pectin]]–[[hemicellulose]] or [[lignin]]–hemicellulose. The third way is to consider the cellular structure of the trunk, based on the mechanical properties, [[density]], and shape of the constituent cells.{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4503}}

==== Properties ====
Increased density (mass per unit of volume) and [[diameter]] (thickness of trunk) are proportional to increased mechanical properties, including [[stiffness]] and [[Strength (material)|strength]].{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4503}}{{Sfn|Lachenbruch|McCulloh|2014|p=751}} In higher density trunks, failure from bending (as a result of wind forces, for example) is more likely to occur from [[Tension (physics)|tensile]] (pulling) fracture than in lower density trunks, where [[buckling]] will most likely occur. The [[fibre saturation point]] is the moisture level at which further drying has limited effect on mechanical properties. Up until this point, decreasing moisture content increases properties in wood the same way as increased density.{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4504}} In response to winds or other mechanical [[Stimulus (physiology)|stimuli]], plants alter their growth through [[thigmomorphogenesis]]. The principle factor that affects the properties, resulting in increased stiffness, is the increase in radius this generates.{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4505}} Another key property of trunks is how hollow they are. Less hollow trees are less likely to buckle and more likely to fail through fracture or [[Yield (engineering)|yielding]].{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4505}} Junctions where [[branch]]es come out of the trunk are the weakest points because they cause a wood-structure called a [[Knot (timber)|knot]].{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4505}}{{Sfn|Lachenbruch|McCulloh|2014|p=752}} The contribution that bark has to structural stiffness is minimal.{{sfn|Shah|Reynolds|Ramage|2017|p=4501}}

=== Dynamics ===
When woody plants oscillate in the wind, there is a risk that they will do so at a [[Resonance|resonant]] frequency (yielding the maximum response), which may lead to branches falling off or even [[Tree uprooting|uprooting]]. The risk is high because they naturally vibrate at a frequency similar to that of the [[Turbulence|turbulent]] wind's resonance (at peak energy).{{sfn|Gardiner|Berry|Moulia|2016|p=102}} Although the canopy provides most of the damping effect (lowering the oscillation), [[structural damping]] is also of significance. In trees it involves the movement of energy, away from the critical trunk and towards the smaller branches and branchlets. The similarities in [[Natural frequency|natural frequencies]] in each part of the tree is what enables this. The net effect of these strategies is oscillation damping, which is valuable because it does not require the tree area (and so wind forces) to increase.{{sfn|Gardiner|Berry|Moulia|2016|p=102}}

