The ecology of living tree trunks is inseparable from the ecology of the trees themselves. Where a tree supports a rich ecology, its trunk does also, by providing key structural and nutritional functions. Tree trunks support plants, like [[epiphyte]]s which grow directly on the tree,{{Sfn|Zotz|2016|p=1}} as well as [[invertebrate]]s and animals.{{Sfn|Zotz|2016|p=219}}

=== Dead ===
{{Main|Coarse woody debris#Benefits}}
When a tree dies, as a result of, for example, wind, fire, disease, [[insect]]s, or suppression, it becomes coarse woody debris (CWD). This takes the form of dead standing trees, fallen tree trunks, large branches, or chunks of wood.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|pp=133–134}} Later it will turn into fine woody debris. CWD's ecological value is extensive, as demonstrated by its use as a [[habitat]] (place for animals to live), establishment of [[seedling]]s, [[Nutrient cycle|nutrient cycling]], [[nitrogen fixation]], food value, and [[sediment transport]] in rivers.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|pp=134–135|1986}}{{sfn|Graham|1925|p=397}}

There are several mechanisms by which CWD [[Decomposition|decomposes]], thus contributing to nutrient cycling. These are: leaching, where water diffuses through CWD and removes minerals;{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|p=149|1986}} fragmentation, mechanically both by animals and plants;{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|p=150|1986}} transport in rivers, both mechanically and microbially; collapse, when the tree cannot support its own weight;{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|p=150|1986}} [[Respiration (physiology)|respiration]], performed by microbes;{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|pp=151–152}} and biological transformation, where CWD is [[Metabolism|metabolised]] (broken down) by microbes and invertebrates.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|pp=152–153}} Decomposition is affected by factors including: temperature, moisture, [[oxygen]] and [[carbon dioxide]] levels, nutrient quality of the CWD, size, and organisms present.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|pp=168–188}}{{sfn|Graham|1925|p=408}} CWD represents a significant fraction of all above ground nutrient, carbon, and organic matter storage.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|p=247}}

CWD is a critical [[Substrate (biology)|substrate]] (living surface) for [[autotroph]]s (plants, [[algae]], bacteria etc.) and serves many important ecological roles for them. Autotrophs known to use CWD are many and varied and include: [[lichen]]s, [[liverwort]]s, algae, [[fern]]s, [[clubmoss]]es, and both angiosperms and gymnosperms.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|p=218}} CWD may provide: just a living surface, for epiphytes; nutrient value for their roots, both from the CWD and on top of the CWD; shade; and preventing of material flowing down hills.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|pp=218–219}} CWD is used by many animals as a habitat for a variety of purposes. These include: cover, [[Eating|feeding]], [[reproduction]] and, to a lesser extent, resting, [[sleeping]], as bridges, and for both roosting and [[hibernation]].{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|p=226}} Animals recorded using CWD in these ways are varied and include: birds, [[bat]]s, as well as [[reptile]]s, [[amphibian]]s, and fish.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|p=225}}{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|p=233}} The orientation, size, and shape of CWD affects if and how these animals use it.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|p=225}}

CWD has [[Geomorphology|geomorphic]] (landform) impacts on both hills and waterways, as well as impacts on the transportation of [[soil]] and [[sediment]].{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|p=261}} Uprooted trees mix and enrich the soil, and logs act to block the movement of soil, water, and sediment down hill.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|p=262}} In waterways, CWD has an influence on their size and shape, and plays a crucial role in storing sediment.{{sfn|Harmon|Franklin|Swanson|Sollins|1986|pp=263–265}}

== Dating ==
[[File:Tilia_tomentosa_coupe_MHNT.jpg|thumb|Cross section of ''[[Tilia tomentosa]]'', the Silver lime, showing annual rings]]
{{Main|Dendrochronology|Dendroclimatology}}
Tree rings can be used both to [[Chronological dating|date]] the age of a tree, using dendrochronology, and to understand the climate under which the tree lived, through dendroclimatology. In dendrochronology, with the exception of trees grown in specific environments (such as near the equator) and under certain pressures (drought), each tree ring generally represents a period of one year of growth.{{Sfn|Wiedenhoeft|Miller|2005|p=15}}

In dendroclimatology, the influence of climate on the nature of each annual ring is analysed. Two key measurements are the total width of the ring and the maximum density of the latewood.{{Sfn|Hughes|Swetnam|Diaz|2011|p=18}} Higher latewood densities and ring widths correspond to higher average summer temperatures.{{Sfn|Hughes|Swetnam|Diaz|2011|p=29}}

== Uses ==
{{See also|Tree#Uses}}
Products derived from tree trunks such as timber have been used by humans in construction and a myriad of other ways for thousands of years. It is the only major building material that is grown, and is therefore broadly [[Sustainability|sustainable]], and is strong—especially in compression.{{Sfn|Ramage|Burridge|Busse-Wicher|Fereday|2017|p=333}} Beyond construction and a plethora of wooden products, including paper,{{Sfn|Seth|2003|p=351}} it is used also as [[wood fuel]] to heat homes, for power generation, and to make charcoal.{{Sfn|Ramage|Burridge|Busse-Wicher|Fereday|2017|p=352}}{{Sfn|Seth|2003|p=349}} [[Resin]]s, which are exuded by plants, can be harvested and used in products such as [[varnish]]es.{{Sfn|Cunningham|2014|p=2}}{{Sfn|Seth|2003|p=370}} The barks of different trees have a variety of different uses, including: the [[Malaria|antimalarial]] properties of ''[[Cinchona]]''; balloons made from ''[[Wikstroemia]]'' and others, [[fire extinguisher]] foam from ''[[Quillaja saponaria]]''; dying products from [[tannin]]s from ''[[Acacia mearnsii]]'' and others;{{Sfn|Cunningham|2014|p=2}} and [[Cork (material)|cork]] from ''[[Quercus suber]]''.{{Sfn|Cunningham|2014|p=31}}{{Sfn|Seth|2003|p=370}} There are many other medicinal uses of trunks and barks.{{Sfn|Cunningham|2014|p=30}}{{Sfn|Seth|2003|p=339}}{{Sfn|Turner|Ari|p=249|Berkes|Davidson-Hunt|2009}} [[Latex]], which is exuded by some trees, is used to produce [[rubber]]; a flexible and waterproof material.{{sfn|Twiss|1935|p=1075}}

